 ## Overview
-The 3D Chain Code Representation (3DCCR) is a method for describing the surfaces of voxelised solids by encoding boundary faces into a Hierarchical List of Chains (HLoC). It solves the problem of explicitly representing complex voxel geometries, including those with holes, cavities, and multiple components, while supporting edge-connectivity. Unlike methods restricted to solids without holes, this approach generates shorter, more compressible token sequences by pruning redundant paths and restructuring the hierarchy [S1].
+The 3D Chain Code Representation is a method for describing the surfaces of voxelised solids by encoding boundary faces into a Hierarchical List of Chains (HLoC) [S1]. It solves the problem of explicitly representing complex voxel geometries, including those with holes, cavities, and multiple components, while supporting edge-connectivity [S1]. Unlike methods restricted to solids without holes, this approach generates shorter, more compressible token sequences by pruning redundant paths and restructuring the hierarchy [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-**Input:** A voxel space $\mathcal{V}$ of resolution $X \times Y \times Z$, where each voxel $v_{i,j,k}$ has a state $s \in \{0, 1\}$ ($1$ occupied, $0$ empty). The solid $\mathcal{G}$ is assumed to be fully contained within $\mathcal{V}$ (no frontier voxels are part of $\mathcal{G}$) [S1].
-**Output:** A file in the 3D Solid Chain Code (3SCC) format containing the HLoC data structures for all surfaces (outer boundaries and cavities) [S1].
+**Input:** A voxel space $\mathcal{V}$ of resolution $X \times Y \times Z$, where each voxel $v_{i,j,k}$ has a state $s \in \{0, 1\}$ ($1$ occupied, $0$ empty) [S1]. The solid $\mathcal{G}$ is assumed to be fully contained within $\mathcal{V}$ (no frontier voxels are part of $\mathcal{G}$) [S1].
+**Output:** A file in the 3D Solid Chain Code (3SCC) format containing the HLoC data structures for all surfaces [S1].
 
